    "description": "Dena is a mountain landscape in Iran’s Central Zagros, where steep relief supports contrasting woodland, mountain and pastoral environments. The associated UNESCO biosphere reserve provides a framework for considering biodiversity conservation alongside the livelihoods of surrounding communities. [s1][s3]",
    "tags": ["Dena", "Central Zagros", "Mountain ecology", "Biosphere reserves", "Pastoral landscapes"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:42:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:42:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Dena: Mountain Habitats and the Central Zagros | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Dena’s Central Zagros setting, varied vegetation, pastoral connections and the purpose of its UNESCO biosphere reserve."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mountain-and-reserve",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A mountain landscape with several meanings",
        "content": "Dena belongs to the Central Zagros, the mountainous region in western Iran. UNESCO’s account emphasizes steep terrain, snowy winters and strong differences in conditions over short distances. These contrasts help explain why Dena matters as an ecological landscape, beyond its appeal as a mountain destination. [s1]\n\nThe mountain landscape and Dena Biosphere Reserve should be distinguished. A named mountain landscape is a geographical subject; a biosphere reserve is a designated area with conservation and development objectives. UNESCO lists 2010 as Dena’s nomination year and describes core, buffer and transition zones around the Sisakhat, Boyerahmad and Semirum areas. The designation therefore concerns a wider inhabited environment rather than a summit alone. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "vegetation-mosaic",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the vegetation",
        "content": "Oak, wild pistachio and almond feature in UNESCO’s description of Dena, alongside scattered walnut, pear and hackberry trees. Its fauna account includes wild goats and large predators, but supplies no monitoring dates or population estimates. These records establish reported occurrence, not present-day abundance. [s1]\n\nA regional ethnobotanical study places this diversity in a broader setting. It describes cooler northern and northeastern parts of Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad Province and warmer terrain toward the southwest. Open woodland dominated by Persian oak, Quercus brantii, occurs alongside herbaceous mountain communities, vegetation rooted in rocky cliffs, and plants associated with streams. [s2]\n\nThat regional evidence is useful for understanding the variety of habitats around Dena. It is not a complete inventory of the massif: a plant recorded somewhere in the province cannot automatically be assigned to every Dena valley. Likewise, documentation of traditional plant use records cultural knowledge; it does not establish the safety or effectiveness of a remedy. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "people-and-conservation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An inhabited conservation landscape",
        "content": "UNESCO describes livestock migration, weaving and other local production traditions within the reserve’s social landscape. Its account connects ecological diversity with communities whose livelihoods involve movement through mountain environments. [s1]\n\nThe wider Man and the Biosphere Programme combines natural and social sciences, linking ecosystem protection with livelihoods and sustainable development. Applied to Dena, this provides a useful editorial lens: understanding the place requires attention to both habitats and human use. The programme’s objectives should be read as a management framework, however, rather than evidence that every conservation goal has been achieved. [s3]\n\nDena is consequently best approached as a connected mountain environment. Its vegetation, wildlife records and pastoral associations tell different parts of that story. Assessing change requires more specific evidence—dated surveys, clearly defined boundaries and repeated observations—than a general reserve profile can provide. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
