    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s coastal geography includes the forest-associated Caspian shore, islands around the Strait of Hormuz and the capes and bays of the Makran coast. These environments differ in vegetation, landforms and human use, making regional comparison more useful than a single description of an Iranian coastline. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Coastal geography", "Iranian islands", "Caspian coast", "Strait of Hormuz", "Makran", "Mangroves"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:42:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:42:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Islands and Coasts: Caspian, Hormuz and Makran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the contrasting landscapes of Iran’s Caspian coast, southern islands, mangrove waterways and Makran capes and bays."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Start with regional differences",
        "content": "Iran’s coastal landscapes resist a single visual shorthand. Along the Caspian, the Hyrcanian forest region connects shoreline geography with wooded mountain slopes. Around the Strait of Hormuz, satellite imagery shows arid islands beside tidal waterways and mangrove habitat. Farther east, the Makran coast is distinguished by projecting headlands, curved bays and sediment-built shorelines. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nThese contrasts offer a practical way into the subject. The northern coast invites questions about forest continuity and land use. The southern islands show how dry terrestrial landscapes can adjoin productive aquatic habitats. Makran demonstrates that a coast is a changing boundary, shaped by rock strength, uplift, waves and sediment movement. Each region needs its own explanation rather than a shared catalogue of beaches. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "caspian-setting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Caspian coast and its forested hinterland",
        "content": "The term Hyrcanian refers to the broad-leaved forest region extending along the southern and southwestern Caspian in Iran and Azerbaijan. UNESCO’s World Heritage description emphasizes its long ecological history and its role as a refuge for forest species through past climatic changes. The designated property consists of separate components within this broader region; it does not encompass every forest or coastal settlement. [s1]\n\nThis distinction matters when reading a map. A coastal region, a forest ecosystem and a protected property have different boundaries. UNESCO also identifies continuing management concerns, including seasonal grazing and wood collection, and says enforcement requires strengthening. International recognition therefore documents ecological significance and conservation obligations, rather than guaranteeing uniformly effective protection. [s1]\n\nFor an introductory coastal guide, the important connection is between shore and hinterland. Understanding the Caspian landscape means looking beyond the water’s edge to the forests and mountain terrain that give the region much of its environmental character. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "southern-islands",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Islands around the Strait of Hormuz",
        "content": "Qeshm lies parallel to the Iranian mainland in the Strait of Hormuz, separated from it by the Khuran channel, also called the Clarence Strait. The European Space Agency’s Sentinel-2 interpretation describes mostly arid land, a largely rocky coastline, and sandy bays and mudflats in the island’s northwest. The same satellite view identifies nearby Hengam, Larak and Hormuz, placing individual islands within a shared coastal setting. [s2]\n\nThe contrast between dry ground and mangrove waterways is especially instructive. Hara’s channels lie between Qeshm and the mainland, where forest and shallow water form a different environment from the island’s exposed rocky shores. A single island can consequently contain, or adjoin, several kinds of coastal habitat. [s2]\n\nSatellite images are useful for recognizing these broad relationships. They do not by themselves establish water quality, wildlife population trends or habitat condition at the editorial cutoff. Those questions require repeated measurements and field evidence alongside imagery. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hara-and-livelihoods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hara: habitat and working coast",
        "content": "UNESCO describes Hara as a mangrove ecosystem associated with tidal marshes, shallow coves and small islands. Its profile records feeding or breeding habitat for marine wildlife and wintering birds. Such descriptions explain ecological functions; they should not be read as a recent census of each species. [s4]\n\nHara is also a lived-in coastal landscape. UNESCO lists trading, fishing, ship construction, palm cultivation and livestock husbandry among local activities. It notes that freshwater scarcity and saline intrusion constrain cultivation near the shoreline. Water conditions therefore influence livelihoods as well as vegetation. [s4]\n\nThe profile identifies pressures including fuelwood collection, grazing and illegal hunting or fishing. These are documented concerns in an institutional account, but the page provides no dated trend showing whether each pressure is increasing or decreasing. A careful introduction can explain the relationship between habitat and use without claiming a measured conservation outcome. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "makran-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Makran: a coastline shaped by competing processes",
        "content": "Makran extends along the Gulf of Oman in southeastern Iran and southwestern Pakistan. NASA’s interpretation of astronaut photography highlights elongated headlands near Konarak and Pozm, as well as sandy beach ridges around Chabahar Bay. Those ridges preserve evidence of earlier shoreline positions. [s3]\n\nThe resulting landscape reflects several processes operating together. Tectonic uplift influences the headlands, while waves and currents redistribute sediment. Harder rock is more resistant to erosion than the material associated with neighboring bays. Consequently, adjacent stretches of shoreline need not respond identically to environmental change. [s3]\n\nNASA’s account explains that erosion and rising sea level can favor inland shoreline movement, while sediment accumulation can build a coast outward. This is a useful conclusion for the whole directory: islands and coasts are connected landscapes with distinct local histories. Their appearance records processes, and understanding change requires evidence specific to each place. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
